<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THE FULL RATIONALE LIVES HERE, NOT IN <description>. A comment is repo-only and is never
        sent to the Metadata API. It must sit INSIDE the root element - a comment placed ABOVE
        <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with the misleading error
        "unable to find matching parent xml file".

        =====================================================================
        🔴 AMENDED 2026-08-14 (Phase 3, observation 7). THIS FIELD IS NOW PER RECORD TYPE.
        THE TWO PSA RECORD TYPES NO LONGER SHARE ONE VOCABULARY, AND FOUR FILES SAID THEY
        ALWAYS WOULD.
        =====================================================================

        Acquisition_PSA runs Draft -> Negotiation -> Signed -> Executed.
        Disposition_PSA runs Initial Draft -> Revised -> Ready for Execution -> Executed,
        COMPLETELY UNCHANGED.

        🔴 NOTHING IS DEACTIVATED ON THIS FIELD, AND THAT IS A HARD STOP, NOT A PREFERENCE
        (decision O3). 'Initial Draft', 'Revised' and 'Ready for Execution' are being retired
        from the ACQUISITION RECORD TYPE ONLY. They remain ACTIVE on the field because
        Disposition_PSA still runs on all three. Deactivating any of them deletes the sell-side
        sequence. Contrast LOI__c.Stage__c in this same phase, where the four retired values ARE
        deactivated at D3 because no record type still uses them. The two fields look like the
        same change and are not.

        🔴 THE THREE NEW VALUES ARE INSERTED BEFORE 'Executed', NOT APPENDED, AND THIS IS THE
        ONE THING IN THIS FILE THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG. Runtime Path order comes from THIS
        file's value order (sorted=false) filtered by the record type; a record type can only
        include or exclude, never re-order. 'Executed' is SHARED and already sits at position 4.
        Appending would have produced, for Acquisition_PSA:
            Executed(4), Draft(5), Negotiation(6), Signed(7)
        i.e. THE TERMINAL STEP RENDERED FIRST, on a Path that would still have deployed green
        and passed every test. Inserting gives:
            Initial Draft(1), Revised(2), Ready for Execution(3),
            Draft(4), Negotiation(5), Signed(6), Executed(7)
          Acquisition_PSA -> Draft, Negotiation, Signed, Executed          [correct]
          Disposition_PSA -> Initial Draft, Revised, Ready for Execution,
                             Executed                                      [UNCHANGED]
        ⚠ CONTRAST LOI__c.Stage__c IN THIS SAME PHASE, where an APPEND was sufficient. The
        difference is where the SHARED value sits: on LOI__c the shared value ('Under Review')
        happens to occupy a position correct for both sequences; here the shared value is the
        TERMINAL. Redo this arithmetic for every future value; the answer does not transfer even
        between two fields changed on the same day. This is the same interleave-vs-append
        question NDA__c answered a third way ('Not Sent' had to move AHEAD of 'Sent').

        THE FIELD-LEVEL DEFAULT STAYS 'Initial Draft' AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT MOVED.
        Disposition_PSA needs it, and Acquisition_PSA supplies its own record-type default
        ('Draft'). Same shape as LOI__c.Stage__c, whose field default is 'Draft' while
        Disposition_LOI defaults to 'Received'.
        🔴 A RECORD-TYPE DEFAULT DOES NOT APPLY TO APEX DML. OpportunityReviewService's
        Contract Review insert stamps NO Negotiation_Status__c and takes the FIELD default, so
        from the moment Acquisition_PSA stops exposing 'Initial Draft' every auto-created
        acquisition PSA is born on a value that is not on its own record type - which commits
        SILENTLY, because record-type picklist restriction is UI-ONLY and is not enforced by
        Apex DML. That is requirements C3 and it is DEVELOPER work, not fixable in this file.
        Do NOT try to fix it by moving the field default - that breaks Disposition_PSA.

        DOWNSTREAM READERS THAT WERE CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED:
          - flows/Contract_Review_Stage_Sync - a BEFORE-SAVE flow with a DEFAULT branch and no
            record-type criterion. 'Draft' would have fallen to that default and shown coarse
            Stage__c = 'Review' on a brand-new acquisition PSA instead of 'PSA Drafting'.
            FIXED in this change: it learned 'Draft' while KEEPING 'Initial Draft'.
            'Negotiation' and 'Signed' are deliberately left to the default - they are the exact
            analogues of 'Revised' and 'Ready for Execution', which also fall to it.
          - flows/PSA_Ready_Notify - entry criterion Negotiation_Status__c = 'Ready for
            Execution', no record-type criterion. Left alone, the "PSA ready for execution"
            notification would have stopped firing for EVERY acquisition deal, permanently and
            silently. FIXED in this change: it now fires on 'Ready for Execution' OR 'Signed'.
          - flexipages/Contract_Review_Record_Page - both Advance Stage entries gate on
            Negotiation_Status__c NE 'Executed'. UNCHANGED AND CORRECT: unlike LOI__c, the PSA
            TERMINAL DID NOT MOVE, so the test still names the right value on both record types.
          - ContractExecutionService keys on 'Executed', which is untouched.
          - objectTranslations/Contract_Review__c-en_US/Negotiation_Status__c still lists the two
            long-removed 'Seller Counter' / 'Buyer Counter' values and has never been maintained
            against this field; it is NOT part of any Phase 3 deploy and gains no new values.

        ================================================================================
        🔴 READ THIS BEFORE REASONING FROM "THE LOI CASE" - PHASE 3 SWAPPED THE TWO OBJECTS'
        PROPERTIES, SO THE ANALOGY NOW POINTS AT THE INVERSE OF THE FACTS.
        ================================================================================

        LOI__c.Stage__c and Contract_Review__c.Negotiation_Status__c are the acquisition /
        disposition sibling pair, and repo prose has long reasoned from one to the other. On
        2026-08-14 they EXCHANGED the property every one of those arguments rests on:

            BEFORE 2026-08-14                      AFTER
            LOI__c.Stage__c        DISJOINT   ->   OVERLAPS at 'Under Review'
                                                       [FROM P3-D1]
            Negotiation_Status__c  IDENTICAL  ->   DISJOINT except the terminal 'Executed'
                                                       [ONLY FROM P3-D3]

        🔴 THE TWO HALVES DO NOT LAND TOGETHER, AND THE WINDOW BETWEEN THEM IS THE NEAR-TERM
        STATE - NOTHING IN THIS PACK IS DEPLOYED YET. The LOI overlap arrives at P3-D1, with the
        three new values. THIS field's disjointness arrives only at P3-D3: the additive step
        leaves Acquisition_PSA carrying SEVEN values, including 'Initial Draft', 'Revised' and
        'Ready for Execution', every one of which Disposition_PSA also carries and keeps. FOR
        THE WHOLE D1 -> D3 WINDOW THE TWO PSA SETS ARE NOT DISJOINT AND A STATUS-KEYED RULE ON
        THIS OBJECT IS NOT SELF-LIMITING. The "AFTER" row is the post-migration end state, not
        the state at the moment this pack deploys - and this block exists to stop someone
        reasoning from an inverted premise, so reading it without that qualifier makes it do the
        opposite of its job for the period it will actually be read in.
        ⚠ Same qualifier, same wording, in flexipages/Contract_Review_Record_Page: "from P3-D3 a
        rule keyed on any NON-terminal status value here WOULD be self-limiting". The transitional
        seven-value state is recorded in recordTypes/Acquisition_PSA and in
        pathAssistants/Contract_Review_Path_Acquisition.

        WHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA. "The sets are disjoint, so a rule keyed on a
        stage value is SELF-LIMITING to its record type" was TRUE of LOI and FALSE here; it is
        FALSE of LOI from P3-D1 and TRUE here only from P3-D3. Anyone carrying that sentence in
        either direction after
        this date carries the exact opposite of the facts, and it fails SILENTLY - a visibility
        rule that stops being self-limiting renders a button on the wrong record type, and one
        that becomes self-limiting hides it. Neither errors and no test sees it.

        WHAT IT COST ALREADY: LOI_Record_Page's Submit_for_Approval entry keys on a value that is
        now shared and carries an OPEN, UNDECIDED residual (runbook 1.6, gate G7).
        Contract_Review_Record_Page went the other way - a stage-keyed rule WOULD now work there,
        and the option was assessed and DECLINED in favour of the custom-permission check.

        ⇒ RULE: never inherit a disjointness claim across this object pair. Re-derive it from the
        two record type files at the moment you need it. It has already inverted once.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-21. THE USER CHANGED Disposition_PSA.Negotiation_Status__c DIRECTLY
        IN SETUP (full measurement: objects/Contract_Review__c/recordTypes/
        Disposition_PSA.recordType-meta.xml's own header). SIX CLAIMS IN THIS FILE'S EARLIER TEXT
        ARE NOW FALSE. RETRACTED HERE, VERBATIM, RATHER THAN DELETED, PER STANDING CONVENTION.
        =====================================================================================

        Disposition_PSA now runs Initial Draft -&gt; Negotiation -&gt; Signed. 'Revised' and
        'Ready for Execution' are removed from it; so is 'Executed' - the terminal is 'Signed'.
        Acquisition_PSA is CONFIRMED UNCHANGED (re-verified live via the UI API immediately before
        this edit).

        RETRACTED (1), verbatim: "Disposition_PSA runs Initial Draft -&gt; Revised -&gt; Ready
        for Execution -&gt; Executed, COMPLETELY UNCHANGED." It changed.

        RETRACTED (2), verbatim, from the "NOTHING IS DEACTIVATED" paragraph: "'Initial Draft',
        'Revised' and 'Ready for Execution' are being retired from the ACQUISITION RECORD TYPE
        ONLY. They remain ACTIVE on the field because Disposition_PSA still runs on all three."
        Disposition_PSA now runs on only ONE of the three ('Initial Draft'). 'Revised' and 'Ready
        for Execution' sit on the field but on NO record type as of this change - the same
        orphaned-value shape LOI__c.Stage__c calls out under its own gate G3. NOT deactivated in
        this pass either; that is still a decision for whoever revisits decision O3, not implied
        by this retraction.

        RETRACTED (3), verbatim, from the ordering-arithmetic diagram: "Disposition_PSA -&gt;
        Initial Draft, Revised, Ready for Execution, Executed [UNCHANGED]." It is now Initial
        Draft, Negotiation, Signed - master positions 1, 5, 6, still monotonic, so no re-order was
        needed to fix this file's own valueSet (which did not change) or the record type's
        inclusion list (which did, in the record type file only).

        RETRACTED (4), verbatim, from the DOWNSTREAM READERS list: "'Negotiation' and 'Signed' are
        deliberately left to the default - they are the exact analogues of 'Revised' and 'Ready
        for Execution', which also fall to it." True for Acquisition_PSA only, now. For
        Disposition_PSA, 'Signed' is the terminal, not a mid-sequence analogue, and FIXED
        2026-08-21: flows/Contract_Review_Stage_Sync gained a new rule, Is_Disposition_Signed
        (Negotiation_Status__c = 'Signed' AND Disposition__c IS NOT NULL), routing that case to
        Stage__c = 'Contract Execution' instead of the default 'Review'. Acquisition's mapping is
        byte-identical to before - the new rule cannot match an acquisition row.

        RETRACTED (5), verbatim, from the flows/PSA_Ready_Notify bullet: "entry criterion
        Negotiation_Status__c = 'Ready for Execution', no record-type criterion... FIXED in this
        change: it now fires on 'Ready for Execution' OR 'Signed'." That was the 2026-08-14 fix,
        and it stopped being sufficient the moment 'Signed' became reachable by Disposition_PSA
        too. FIXED AGAIN 2026-08-21: the flow gained a third entry filter, Opportunity__c IS NOT
        NULL, AND'd with the existing OR, restoring (not changing) its acquisition-only-in-practice
        behaviour now that the premise which made it acquisition-only BY ACCIDENT is gone. See
        that flow's own header for the full reasoning, including why the discriminator is the
        lookup and not RecordType.DeveloperName.

        RETRACTED (6), verbatim, from the flexipages/Contract_Review_Record_Page bullet: "both
        Advance Stage entries gate on Negotiation_Status__c NE 'Executed'. UNCHANGED AND CORRECT:
        unlike LOI__c, the PSA TERMINAL DID NOT MOVE, so the test still names the right value on
        both record types." The PSA terminal DID move, for Disposition_PSA. FIXED 2026-08-21: that
        page's Disposition_Deal_Actions-gated Advance_Stage entry now tests NE 'Signed'; the
        Acquisition_Deal_Actions entry is untouched and still correctly tests NE 'Executed'.
    -->
    <fullName>Negotiation_Status__c</fullName>
    <description>PSA lifecycle, PER RECORD TYPE from 2026-08-14 and amended again 2026-08-21 (hand-mirrored Setup edit). Acquisition_PSA runs Draft -&gt; Negotiation -&gt; Signed -&gt; Executed. Disposition_PSA now runs Initial Draft -&gt; Negotiation -&gt; Signed - terminal is Signed, not Executed, not the old Ready for Execution -&gt; Executed pair. Revised and Ready for Execution stay ACTIVE on the field (decision O3) but are on no record type. Moved ONLY by the deal driver via Advance Stage. Synced to the coarse Stage__c by Contract_Review_Stage_Sync, which gained a Disposition_PSA-scoped branch for Signed on 2026-08-21; drives the Contract Review Paths. See the XML comment in this file.</description>
